Source package: fastflowlm

FastFlowLM runs language models on the NPU of AMD Ryzen AI processors. It publishes .deb files of its releases on
GitHub, but no source package, so it is rebuilt from its git repository: source-git names it, and the newest tag
named like a version is cloned with its submodules (third_party/tokenizers-cpp and third_party/xgrammar). The source
carries its own debian/, a 3.0 (native) package built with CMake, and a debian/changelog is written for the tag's
version, because the repository has only a template of it, which FastFlowLM's CI fills in. The version the program
reports comes from src/CMakePresets.json, not from the changelog, so the rebuild reports the release's version.

The NPU engines are prebuilt libraries in src/lib/xrt and the NPU kernels prebuilt files in src/xclbins, under AMD's
FastFlowLM NPU Binary License, which allows redistributing them unmodified. The rest is MIT licensed. The patches
change only the MIT licensed part, which the build compiles and links with the prebuilt libraries.

The build downloads the sources of the image and audio codecs, which CMake checks against their SHA256, and the Rust
crates of the tokenizers, whose versions cargo resolves at build time, as no Cargo.lock pins them, and whose build
scripts it runs. So sbuild-options gives the build network access, and ca-certificates for HTTPS. It is the one
package built with network access. FastFlowLM's own CI builds its packages the same way.

prefix-cache-shared-history.patch
    The server's prefix cache saves the model's state while it reads a prompt, so that a later prompt which starts
    the same way continues from that state instead of reading everything again. It saved a snapshot at the end of
    the system prompt and tools, only when that is at least 512 tokens long, and one at the end of every prompt. The
    recurrent state of Qwen3.5, Qwen3.6 and Qwen3.8 cannot be rewound to an earlier position, so a prompt continues
    only from a snapshot whose tokens begin it. Prompts which share everything but their last user message, like the
    same few-shot examples followed by different inputs, therefore read the examples again every time.

    The patch saves a snapshot at the start of the last user message too, as a boundary snapshot, when the prompt before
    shared everything up to it and went on with another last message. Reading up to that point is a prefill pass of its
    own, which on qwen3.6-moe:35b-a3b costs about 3.5 seconds whatever its length. A chat turn only continues the turn
    before and never reuses that point, and would pay for the pass every turn, so it is saved only when another prompt
    went on from it. It is saved when it is at least 512 tokens into the prompt and at least --prefix-min-gain tokens
    (128 by default) past the end of the system prompt and tools and past what the prompt resumed from. The end
    snapshots saved after it hold only the rows after it. Qwen3.8, which keeps only the shared start of a conversation,
    keeps this one as well.

    The patch adds two tests to src/test/prefix_slot_cache, and updates the documentation of the prefix cache in
    docs/. It is meant to be proposed upstream as it is, and has not been yet.

keep-prebuilt-libraries.patch (debian-series)
    dpkg-source leaves files named like build output, *.so and *.a among them, out of a source package of format 3.0
    by default, so a source package built from the tree lacked the prebuilt NPU engines in src/lib, and its build
    failed to link. debian/source/options with an explicit tar-ignore replaces that default list. FastFlowLM's own
    CI builds only binary packages, straight from a checkout, and never a source package.

cargo-home-in-build-tree.patch (debian-series)
    Cargo keeps the crates it downloads in its home, which defaults to .cargo in the home directory. sbuild sets HOME
    to a directory which does not exist, so debian/rules points CARGO_HOME into the build tree.

run-prefix-cache-tests.patch (debian-series)
    The tests of the prefix cache are a CMake project of their own, which the build does not run. They need no NPU, so
    debian/rules runs them, unless DEB_BUILD_OPTIONS has nocheck.

After a version bump, check that the tests still exercise _prefix_insert as src/common/AutoModel/automodel.cpp does:
the prefill() helper of the test mirrors it.
